# {{.Service}}

Kitex service generated by devkit.

```sh
make tools        # once per machine: kitex + thriftgo
make gen          # regenerate kitex_gen/ (and kitex_gen/errcode) from the IDLs listed in idl.mk; locks the framework's files read-only afterwards
make run          # conf/local.yaml: your machine, with the Nacos on it (the file says how to start one)
make lint         # devkit lint; the pre-push hook runs it with make test before every push
make test         # one test: make test RUN=TestXxx
make verify       # the post-deploy checks (app/verify.go plus the framework's) against conf/local.yaml
```

- IDL lives in the project's shared IDL repository (`../idl` next to this
  service, `../idl/{{.Service}}/{{.Service}}.thrift`). Change it there, run
  `make gen`, implement the new method, and push the IDL before the service.
- `handler/` is one package split by topic: `handler.go` holds the struct,
  `New` and `Ping` only; `codes.go` holds constants this service alone uses;
  RPC methods go in `handler/<topic>.go` (a noun: `profile.go`, `order.go`),
  together with that topic's event consumers and delayed-task handlers; tests
  next to them in package `handler_test`. `devkit lint` (rule `handler-split`)
  keeps it that way.
- A Resp carries data only. A business failure is
  `return nil, errcode.Err(errcode.<Name>)`: the codes are declared in
  `../idl/errors/<domain>.thrift` and generated into `kitex_gen/errcode` with
  their messages; the gateway adds the `{code, msg, data}` envelope.
- What is yours and what is the framework's: `app/` (`app.go` wiring,
  `jobs.go` scheduled jobs, `verify.go` post-deploy checks), `handler/`,
  `repo/`, `conf/*.yaml`, `idl.mk`, `go.mod` and this file are yours and never
  rewritten. `cmd/{{.Service}}/main.go`, the Makefile, the CI files, the
  Dockerfile, `conf/README*.md`, `kitex_gen/`, `.idl/`, `idl.lock` and
  `.devkit/` belong to the framework: `devkit update` replaces them and
  `devkit lock` keeps them read-only on your machine. Service-specific wiring
  (config fields, dependencies, shutdown cleanup, extra server options) goes
  into `app/app.go`.
- To call another service, add its IDL to `idl.mk` (for example
  `ser-order/ser-order.thrift`), run `make gen`, and use the generated client in
  `kitex_gen/`. `idl.mk` is yours; devkit never rewrites it.
- Configuration is `conf/<APP_ENV>.yaml`; `${VAR}` placeholders are read from
  the environment. Four environments: `local` (no `APP_ENV`: your own machine,
  registered in the Nacos on it), `dev` (the shared development server), `uat`
  and `prod`. To work from your machine against the development server's Nacos,
  see `register: false` in `conf/local.yaml`; a laptop never registers there.
- Scheduled jobs go in `app/jobs.go`: a name, a cron schedule (UTC) and a
  function. `make jobs` lists them, `make job NAME=<name>` runs one here. The
  deployment turns the list into one Kubernetes CronJob per job; a run is a
  root trace and a log with its `run_id`, locked against overlap and cancelled
  at its timeout.
- The rules: how to add a method, a table, an event, a job or a post-deploy
  check is in the IDL repository's `AGENTS.md`; the interface and event
  conventions in its `README.md`; every framework capability in
  `{{.CommonModule}}` (checked out read-only at `../kit-common/`, see its
  `README.zh-CN.md`).
